  "description": "Factorize real symmetric matrix A into orthogonal eigenvector matrix V and diagonal eigenvalue matrix Lambda.\n\nThe Spectral Theorem guarantees that any real symmetric matrix has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{V} \\mathbf{\\Lambda} \\mathbf{V}^T, \\quad \\mathbf{V} \\mathbf{V}^T = \\mathbf{I}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Compute eigenvalues and orthogonal eigenvectors using QR algorithm\n2. Sort eigenvalue spectrum in descending order\n3. Format output dictionary with sorted 'eigenvalues' and 'eigenvectors' rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'eigenvalues' and 'eigenvectors'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Eigendecomposition\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **Eigendecomposition** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nThe Spectral Theorem guarantees that any real symmetric matrix has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. In this problem, the goal is to: factorize real symmetric matrix a into orthogonal eigenvector matrix v and diagonal eigenvalue matrix lambda.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{V} \\mathbf{\\Lambda} \\mathbf{V}^T, \\quad \\mathbf{V} \\mathbf{V}^T = \\mathbf{I}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Compute eigenvalues and orthogonal eigenvectors using QR algorithm\n2. **Phase 2**: Sort eigenvalue spectrum in descending order\n3. **Phase 3**: Format output dictionary with sorted 'eigenvalues' and 'eigenvectors' rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: A = [[2.0, 1.0], [1.0, 2.0]]\n\nOutput: {\"eigenvalues\": [3.0, 1.0], \"eigenvectors\": [[0.7071, -0.7071], [0.7071, 0.7071]]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated la_eigendecomposition under standard test conditions."
